Recursion — One-Liners

    What:
        Recursion is a function calling itself to solve smaller instances of the same problem.
    Why:
        To simplify complex, self-similar problems by breaking them into smaller parts.
    Where:
        Used in trees, graphs, backtracking, divide-and-conquer, and nested structures.
    When:
        When a problem can be reduced into smaller identical subproblems with a clear base case.
    How:
        By defining a base case and repeatedly calling the function with reduced input until it stops.
        
Ultra-Short Memory Version

    What  → function calls itself
    Why   → simplify complex problems
    Where → trees, graphs, recursion-heavy problems
    When  → self-similar breakdown exists
    How   → base case + recursive call


Recursion(Self-calling function)

Looping   -> Repetition

    CPU executes instructions repeatedly
    No new memory allocation
    
    Scrolling Instagram
    
        Scroll → see post → scroll → repeat
        Stops when you close app
        
    Other Examples:
    
        Walking Steps Counter
        Eating Food
        Traffic Signal System
        Factory assembly line 
        
Recursion:

    Each call → pushed into stack
    Memory grows
    Then shrinks when returning
    Recursion -> Self-calling function(self-repetition)

    Examples: 
    
        Opening Nested Boxes 
        Family tree 
        Mirror facing Mirror
        File system navigation 
        Solving Maze 
    
Loop = LINEAR THINKING

    Do → Repeat → Stop
    You control repetition
    Loop → "Again and again"
    
Recursion = HIERARCHICAL THINKING

    Solve → smaller version → smaller version → base case
    Problem controls itself
    Recursion → "Inside itself"
    
    
Loop = explicit control -> outside control
Recursion = implicit control (stack manages it) -> inside control


Loop:
    Climbing stairs step by step
Recursion:
    Opening a staircase inside a staircase inside a staircase



