#include<stdio.h>
double taylor(int x , int n)
{
    static double Exp = 1.0;
    static double fac = 1.0;
    double res = 1.0;
    if(n == 0) return 1;
    else 
    {
        res = taylor(x , n - 1);
        Exp = Exp * x;
        fac = fac * n;
        return (Exp/fac) + res;
    }
}
int main()
{
    int x;
    int n;
    scanf("%d %d",&x, &n);
    printf("%f",taylor(x , n));
}


Looping: 

1. CPU Scheduling (Operating System)
    CPU runs:
    Process A → Process B → Process C → repeat
    Happens millions of times per second
    This is a high-speed loop
    while system_running:
        execute_next_process()
        
2. Heartbeat (Biological Loop)
    Heart beats continuously
    Lub → Dub → repeat
    Stops only when condition fails (life ends)
    
3. Washing Machine Cycle
    Fill water → wash → rinse → spin
    Repeats cycles
    Controlled loop with stages
    
4. CCTV Monitoring System
    Camera continuously records frames
    while camera_on:
        capture_frame()
        
5. Game Loop (Very Important in Programming)
    Every game runs on loop:
    Input → Update → Render → repeat
    
    
Recursion:

1. Trees in Nature (Fractals)
    Branch → smaller branches → smaller branches
    Same pattern repeating at smaller scale
    
2. Google Maps Zoom System
    World map → country → city → street → building
    Each level is a smaller version of same structure
    
3. Company Hierarchy
    CEO → Managers → Employees
    Each manager manages a sub-tree
    Recursive structure
    
4. Brain Thinking (Breaking Problems)
    Big problem → smaller problem → smaller → solve
    That’s recursion thinking
    
5. Website DOM Structure (HTML)
    Page contains:
    Div → contains div → contains div
    Nested structure = recursion

